For a linear network, the \(V\)-\(I\) characteristic can be represented by its Thevenin equivalent. <img src="img/b87e969b283bf16a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,160px)"> From the graph, \[V_{OC}=20\text{ V}, \qquad I_{SC}=4\text{ A}\] Therefore, \[R_{TH}
=
\frac{V_{OC}}{I_{SC}}
=
\frac{20}{4}
=
5\Omega\] and \[V_{TH}=V_{OC}=20\text{ V}\] So the Thevenin equivalent circuit is; <img src="img/aee5cf646dcaa772.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,147px)"> For maximum power transfer, \[R_L=R_{TH}=5\Omega\] Hence the load current is \[I
=
\frac{V_{TH}}{R_{TH}+R_L}
=
\frac{20}{5+5}
=
2\text{ A}\] Therefore, \[P_{\max}
=
I^2R_L
=
(2)^2(5)
=
20\text{ W}\] Or, \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{V_{TH}^{2}}{4R_{TH}}
=
\frac{20^2}{4\times5}
=
20\text{ W}\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{C. }20\text{ W}}\]

The characteristic equation is given by \(1 + G(s) = 0\): \[1 + \frac{K(s + 2)^2}{s^2(s - 1)} = 0 \implies s^3 - s^2 + K(s^2 + 4s + 4) = 0\] \[s^3 + (K - 1)s^2 + 4Ks + 4K = 0\] RH Array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 4K \\
s^2 &amp; K - 1 &amp; 4K \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{4K(K - 1) - 4K}{K - 1} = \dfrac{4K^2 - 8K}{K - 1} &amp; 0 \\
s^0 &amp; 4K &amp; 
\end{array}\] For stable operation, all elements in the first column must be strictly positive: <ul><li>From \(s^2\) row: \(K - 1 &gt; 0 \implies K &gt; 1\)</li><li>From \(s^1\) row: \(4K^2 - 8K &gt; 0 \implies 4K(K - 2) &gt; 0 \implies K &gt; 2\)</li></ul> \[\boxed{K &gt; 2}\]

From the given Bode plot, the initial slope is \(-20\,\text{dB/dec}\). Hence, there is one pole at the origin. At \(\omega=0.05\,\text{rad/sec}\), the slope changes from \(-20\,\text{dB/dec}\) to \(-40\,\text{dB/dec}\). Hence, there is one pole at \(\omega_p=0.05\) rad/sec. At \(\omega=0.1\,\text{rad/sec}\), the slope changes from \(-40\,\text{dB/dec}\) to \(-20\,\text{dB/dec}\). Hence, there is one zero at \(\omega_z=0.1\) rad/sec. Therefore, the transfer function can be written as \[G(s)=
K\frac{\left(1+\dfrac{s}{0.1}\right)}
{s\left(1+\dfrac{s}{0.05}\right)}\] For \(\omega&lt;0.05\,\text{rad/sec}\), neither finite pole nor zero is activated. Therefore, \[G(j\omega)\approx \frac{K}{j\omega}\] At \(\omega=0.01\,\text{rad/sec}\), magnitude from the plot is \(60\,\text{dB}\). \[20\log\left|\frac{K}{j0.01}\right|=60\] \[20\log(100K)=60\] \[K=10\] Hence, \[G(s)=
10\frac{\left(1+\dfrac{s}{0.1}\right)}
{s\left(1+\dfrac{s}{0.05}\right)}
=
\frac{5(s+0.1)}
{s(s+0.05)}
=
\frac{10(1+10s)}{s(1+20s)}\] Therefore, the correct options are \[\boxed{(B)\;\frac{10(1+10s)}{s(1+20s)}}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;\frac{5(s+0.1)}{s(s+0.05)}}\]

Let, \[V_{1}=200\,\text{V},\qquad V_{2}=160\,\text{V}\] \[f_{1}=50\,\text{Hz},\qquad f_{2}=40\,\text{Hz}\] For a single-phase transformer: Eddy current loss is, \[P_{e}\propto f^{2}B_{m}^{2}\] Since, \[B_{m}\propto \frac{V}{f}\] Substituting: \[\frac{P_{e1}}{P_{e2}}
=
\frac{f_{1}^{2}B_{m1}^{2}}
{f_{2}^{2}B_{m2}^{2}}
=
\frac{V_{1}^{2}}{V_{2}^{2}}\] \[\frac{P_{e2}}{P_{e1}}
=
\left(\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}\right)^2\] \[=
\left(\frac{160}{200}\right)^2
=
0.64\] Hence, \[\text{Percentage reduction in eddy current loss}
=
(1-0.64)\times100=36\%\] Hysteresis loss: \[P_{h}\propto B_{m}^{1.6}f\] Substituting, \[P_{h}\propto
\left(\frac{V}{f}\right)^{1.6}f\] \[P_{h}
\propto
V^{1.6}f^{-0.6}\] Thus, \[\frac{P_{h2}}{P_{h1}}
=
\left(\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}\right)^{1.6}
\left(\frac{f_{2}}{f_{1}}\right)^{-0.6}\] \[=
\left(\frac{160}{200}\right)^{1.6}
\left(\frac{40}{50}\right)^{-0.6}=
0.8\] Hence, \[\text{Percentage reduction in hysteresis loss}
=
(1-0.8)\times100\] \[=
20\%\]

Given: \[P=6,\qquad f_s=60\,\text{Hz}\] The synchronous speed is \[N_s=\frac{120f_s}{P}
=\frac{120\times 60}{6}
=1200\,\text{rpm}\] The rotor speed is \[N_r=3000\,\text{rpm}\] For torque production, the rotor and stator magnetic fields must be stationary with respect to each other. <strong>Case 1:</strong><br/>Assume that both rotor field and stator field rotate in the same direction as the rotor. The stator field speed with respect to space is \[N_s=1200\,\text{rpm}\] Hence, \[3000-N_{rf}=1200\] \[N_{rf}=1800\,\text{rpm}\] where \(N_{rf}\) is the rotor field speed with respect to the rotor structure. Using \[N_{rf}=\frac{120f_r}{P}\] \[1800=\frac{120f_r}{6}\] \[f_r=\frac{1800\times 6}{120}
=90\,\text{Hz}\]

When the motor is connected in star, the starting torque is less than that in delta because the phase voltage in star is lower. For equal starting torque in star and delta, \[T_Y=T_\Delta\] Since starting torque is proportional to square of phase current, \[T\propto I_2^2R_2\] Therefore, \[I_2=I_2'\] Here, both \(I_2\) and \(I_2'\) are phase currents. The equivalent per-phase rotor parameters referred to stator are \[R_{eq}=1+1.4=2.4\,\Omega\] \[X_{eq}=4.5+4.5=9\,\Omega\] For star connection, <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[I_2=
\frac{400/\sqrt{3}}
{\sqrt{(R_c+2.4)^2+9^2}}\] \[I_2=
\frac{400/\sqrt{3}}
{\sqrt{(R_c+2.4)^2+81}}\] For delta connection, converting delta impedance to equivalent star, <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[Z_Y=\frac{2.4+j9}{3}\] The line current in the equivalent star circuit is \[(I_2')_Y=
\frac{400/\sqrt{3}}
{\sqrt{\left(R_c+\frac{2.4}{3}\right)^2+3^2}}\] The phase current in delta is \[I_2'=\frac{(I_2')_Y}{\sqrt{3}}\] Therefore, \[I_2'=
\frac{400/3}
{\sqrt{\left(R_c+\frac{2.4}{3}\right)^2+9}}\] Equating currents, \[\frac{400/3}
{\sqrt{\left(R_c+\frac{2.4}{3}\right)^2+9}}
=
\frac{400/\sqrt{3}}
{\sqrt{(R_c+2.4)^2+81}}\] Squaring and simplifying, \[3\left(R_c+\frac{2.4}{3}\right)^2-(R_c+2.4)^2=54\] \[2R_c^2=57.84\] \[R_c=\sqrt{28.92}\] \[R_c=5.377\,\Omega\] Hence, \[\boxed{5.377}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For equal starting torque, rotor phase currents must be equal.</li><li>In delta connection, phase current is line current divided by \(\sqrt{3}\).</li><li>Delta impedance can be converted to equivalent star by dividing by \(3\).</li><li>Starting torque is proportional to \(I_2^2R_2\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not compare line currents directly; compare phase currents.</li><li>Do not forget delta-to-star conversion of impedance.</li><li>Do not omit the feeder resistance \(R_c\) while calculating voltage drop.</li></ul></div>

